   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of Member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On August 01, 2023, Brazil adopted a new UNSDCF, covering the period of 2023 &#x2013; 2027. Relevant ILO topics including issues raised by ILO Supervisory bodies (such as non-discrimination) have been contemplated in consultations to ILO social partners and civil society organizations. These include the promotion of just transition, decent work, social justice, non-discrimination in the workplace, green jobs and safe migration. In addition, the realization of Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work has been mainstreamed.  
The UNSDCF was jointly developed by the Brazilian government and the United Nations System in Brazil. This marks the country&apos;s inaugural UNSDCF and is the outcome of an extensive dialogue process. The ILO social partners were consulted. 
The Framework is structured around five fundamental axes for sustainable development: 
1. Economic transformation for sustainable development, 2. Social inclusion for sustainable development,  3. Environment and climate change for sustainable development,  4. Governance and institutional capacity, and 5. The relationship between humanitarian actions and sustainable development.  
On Axis 1, it is underscoring that economic transformation compatible with the 2030 Agenda must include the promotion of quality work and employment, with productive inclusion that strengthens spaces and mechanisms for social dialogue between government, employers, and workers.  
Axis 2 includes outcomes related to the extension and strengthening of social protection, promotion of human rights and combat discrimination.  
Axis 4 incorporates the issues of social protection and social justice, as a core aspect of inclusion and social dialogue as a vector for participation and consensus-building around public policies. In addition, it includes an outcome on the promotion of South South Cooperation. 
The UNSDCF was signed by the RC (representing the system) and by the Minister of Foreign Affairs. The DSG and the UN heads in Brazil. The ILO provided technical input in the drafting of the Common Country Analysis (CCA) and made core inputs to the text related to Economic Transformation in Brazil. Also, ILO provided analysis in the section relating to Progress on SDG 8 as they relate to issues on labor market situation, especially in terms of informality, inequality, discrimination and violations of fundamental principles and rigths t work. The ILO also participated in the drafting of the UNSDCF that drew upon issues flagged in the CCA and was part of the identification of structural and policy measures required to address such problems (especially on FPRW). Under the 1st strategic priority of the UNSDCF focusing on Economic transformation for sustainable development, the 2nd related to Social inclusion for sustainable development, and the 4th regarding Governance and institutional capacity the ILO made substantive input to the theory of change to underscore the critical link between reducing persistent social and economic inequalities for vulnerable populations, youth and other marginalized groups with outcomes for better living and working under conditions of security and dignity. Worth to mention that ILO indicated members of workers&apos;, employers&#x2019; and civil society organizations to be consulted, as well as government representatives, in the process of consultations prior to the UNSDCF elaboration, to guarantee that their views were taken into consideration in this context.</narrative>
   </description>
